<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  Chapter 9: Understanding Variability                              -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->
<chapter xml:id="ch-variability" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Understanding Variability</title>

    <introduction xml:id="intro-ch9-variability">
        <p>
            Data is variable.
            If you measure the same thing twice, you will rarely get exactly the
            same answer.
            Track the population in a single country over fifty years and the
            numbers will shift, sometimes smoothly, sometimes in sudden jolts.
            This chapter is about variability: what it is, where it comes from,
            and how to measure it.
            In this chapter, we work through the main measures of variability
            using life expectancy data from African countries as our running
            example.
            Each measure answers the question a little differently, and we look
            at what each one picks up and what it misses.
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <section xml:id="sec-variability-how-much">
        <title>How Much Does It Vary?</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                In Nairobi, streets are regularly gridlocked.
                News reports claim that commutes regularly exceed two hours each
                way.
                But is that consistently the case, and for whom is that the case
                for?
                The answer depends entirely on how you measure it, and who you
                ask.
            </p>

            <p>
                In 2013, a World Bank survey of 14,580 households across 15
                Kenyan cities set out to answer this question systematically.
                The researchers noted that while congestion at peak hours was
                obvious to anyone on Nairobi's streets, it was simply not known
                which commuters were experiencing that congestion, or what their
                commute times actually were (Salon and Gulyani, 2019).
                In the end, the survey found that the median commute time in
                Nairobi was 30 minutes, and only 5% of those surveyed reported
                commuting an hour or longer.
                Far from a city of two-hour commutes, most Nairobi residents
                appeared to be getting to work in half an hour.
            </p>

            <p>
                But another major survey, conducted in the same year by JICA,
                found an average commute time of 49 minutes — nearly twice the
                World Bank figure.
                Both surveys measured the same city in the same year.
                The difference largely came down to two things: Firstly, who was
                sampled: the JICA survey had oversampled car-owning households,
                who tend to have longer motorised commutes, while the World Bank
                survey was more representative of the full population — most of
                whom walk or take a matatu.
                Measure the same thing differently, and you get a different
                answer.
                Secondly, Salon and Gulyani (2019) reported a mean, while the
                JICA survey reported a median.
                The mean is more sensitive to outliers, so the two numbers
                aren't directly comparable.
            </p>

            <insight>
                <title>Which Measurement of Spread?</title>

                <p>
                    Part of the difference between JICA and the World Bank
                    results is mean versus median.
                    The mean can be influenced by extreme values.
                    Here, the small number of commuters with very long journeys
                    pulls the average upward, while the median remains more
                    representative of the typical commute.
                    JICA reported a mean of 49 minutes, and a median of 40
                    minutes; while the World Bank reported a median of 30
                    minutes, and a mean of 31 minutes.
                </p>
            </insight>

            <p>
                Even within the World Bank survey, the variation is substantial.
                Matatu commuters in Nairobi spent roughly twice as long
                travelling as pedestrian commuters who made a similar trip in
                the city.
                The median commute time conceals the fact that some people's
                journeys are short and some are long — and who experiences which
                depends on how they travel, where they live, and what time they
                leave.
            </p>

            <p>
                This is what variability looks like in practice.
                A variable, such as commuting time, is in a spread of values
                with a centre, a range, and a shape, called a distribution.
                Understanding that distribution, rather than just its average,
                is what allows planners to know whether interventions are
                helping everyone or only some commuters.
                It is what allows researchers to compare across cities fairly.
                And it is what allows the data analyst to say something honest
                about what the data actually shows.
                This chapter introduces the tools for observing and measuring
                variability.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-commute-histogram-example">
                <caption>
                    A histogram of 500 simulated commuting times.
                    The solid red line marks the mean and the dashed blue line
                    marks the median.
                    A few very long commutes pull the mean above the median.
                </caption>

                <image source="images/ch9_example.png" width="90%">
                    <description>
                        <p>
                            A histogram titled "Commuting times are
                            right-skewed".
                            The horizontal axis shows commuting time in minutes,
                            from 0 to about 160, and the vertical axis shows the
                            number of commuters.
                            Most bars are tall and cluster between about 15 and
                            50 minutes, peaking at around 30 minutes.
                            The bars then fall away gradually, forming a long
                            tail to the right, with a few isolated commuters
                            taking over 100 minutes.
                            A dashed blue vertical line marks the median at
                            about 34 minutes, and a solid red line marks the
                            mean at about 39 minutes, just to the right of the
                            median.
                        </p>
                    </description>
                </image>
            </figure>

            <p>
                <xref ref="fig-commute-histogram-example"/> uses simulated data
                to illustrate what a distribution looks like.
                The mean sits to the right of the median because a few very long
                commutes pull it upwards.
                Two surveys reporting these different measures of the average
                could describe the same commuters and still give different
                numbers.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-traffic-variation-measuring">
            <title>Observing Variability in Nairobi Traffic</title>

            <p>
                The TomTom Traffic Index records journey times across Nairobi
                continuously, aggregating anonymised GPS data from millions of
                trips across the entire road network.
                The data estimated that in 2025, a 10 kilometre drive in the
                city took an average of 26 minutes and 47 seconds.
            </p>

            <p>
                But that single number is already hiding the variability in
                journey times.
                At 4am, the same 10 kilometres takes around 18 minutes.
                At 4pm on a weekday, it takes more than twice as long at around
                37 minutes.
                The average sits between those two extremes, but it does not
                tell you that the extremes exist, or how far apart they are.
            </p>

            <p>
                One of the simplest ways to measure variability is the
                <term>range</term>: the difference between the largest and
                smallest values.
                Here, a 10km journey across the day ranges from roughly
                <c>37 - 18 = 19</c> minutes.
                This is a very useful starting point to gauge an overall idea
                about the variability.
            </p>

            <p>
                However, the range only tells you only about the two most
                extreme values.
                It does not say anything about a typical journey, or how much
                most journeys deviate from the average.
                And it is sensitive to unusual extremes: one exceptionally bad
                hour can increase the range, and hence make the variability
                larger.
            </p>

            <p>
                To get a fuller picture, TomTom also estimated that over the
                entirety of 2025, the average Nairobi driver lost more than four
                and a half days (109 hours) sitting in traffic beyond what those
                same journeys would have taken at free-flow speeds.
            </p>

            <p>
                Variability can also be captured through various graphics; for
                example, <xref ref="fig-tomtom-heatmap-week"/> shows the average
                journey times every two hours and every day of the week in 2025.
                Each cell represents one two-hour slot on one day of the week;
                darker red means longer journey times, lighter pink means
                shorter.
                This is a visual representation of variability: you can see at a
                glance not just that variation exists, but that journeys on
                weekday evenings are typically the longest, and journeys on
                early Sunday mornings are typically the shortest.
                The range from the lightest cell to the darkest is visible
                without any calculation at all.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-tomtom-heatmap-week">
                <caption>
                    Travel time heatmap for Nairobi across all hours and days of
                    the week, averaged across 2025.
                    Darker red indicates longer journey times; lighter pink
                    indicates shorter.
                    Source: TomTom Traffic Index 2025,
                    <url href="https://www.tomtom.com/traffic-index/city/nairobi/" visual="tomtom.com/traffic-index/city/nairobi"/>.
                </caption>

                <image source="images/ch9_tomtom_heatmap_week.png" width="100%">
                    <description>
                        <p>
                            A heatmap with days of the week (Monday to Sunday)
                            across the top and hours of the day down the left
                            side.
                            Cells are coloured from light pink to dark red.
                            The darkest cells appear in the band from
                            approximately 5pm to 7pm on weekdays, particularly
                            Monday to Thursday.
                            Saturday and Sunday are substantially lighter
                            throughout, especially in the morning hours.
                            The 4am slot on Monday is notably light.
                        </p>
                    </description>
                </image>
            </figure>

            <insight>
                <title>If there were no variability</title>

                <p>
                    How would this graphic look if there were no variability?
                    If a 10 kilometre journey took exactly the same time at
                    every hour of every day, every cell would be the same
                    colour.
                    There would be no dark band in the evenings and no light
                    patch on Sunday mornings.
                    The heatmap would be one flat block of colour, and it would
                    tell you nothing more than the average already did.
                    The patterns you can see in
                    <xref ref="fig-tomtom-heatmap-week"/> exist because journey
                    times vary, and the more they vary, the more the colours
                    differ.
                </p>
            </insight>

            <p>
                The same variation can be viewed spatially.
                <xref ref="fig-tomtom-map-morning"/> and
                <xref ref="fig-tomtom-map-evening"/> show average traffic
                conditions during the morning and evening rush hours across the
                city.
                Not all parts of Nairobi are equally congested — the central
                business district and main arterial roads are consistently
                darker, while the outer suburbs are lighter.
                And the spatial pattern of variability shifts between morning
                and evening: congestion concentrates inward as commuters arrive
                in the morning, and spreads outward as they leave in the
                evening.
                The same underlying variation in journey times is now being
                shown as a distribution across space rather than across time.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-tomtom-map-morning">
                <caption>
                    Traffic conditions in Nairobi during the morning rush hour,
                    averaged across 2025.
                    Yellow indicates moderate congestion, orange and red
                    indicate heavier delays.
                    Source: TomTom Traffic Index 2025.
                </caption>

                <image source="images/ch9_tomtom_heatmap_morning.png" width="90%">
                    <description>
                        <p>
                            A hexagonal heatmap overlaid on a dark map of
                            Nairobi.
                            The hexagons covering the central business district
                            and major roads leading into it are coloured orange
                            to red, indicating significant congestion.
                            The outer suburbs and peripheral areas are
                            predominantly yellow, indicating lighter congestion.
                            The pattern shows congestion concentrated in the
                            city centre and along main arterial routes during
                            the morning peak.
                        </p>
                    </description>
                </image>
            </figure>

            <figure xml:id="fig-tomtom-map-evening">
                <caption>
                    Traffic conditions in Nairobi during the evening rush hour,
                    averaged across 2025.
                    Congestion spreads more broadly than in the morning,
                    extending further into residential areas as commuters make
                    their way home.
                    Source: TomTom Traffic Index 2025.
                </caption>

                <image source="images/ch9_tomtom_heatmap_evening.png" width="90%">
                    <description>
                        <p>
                            A hexagonal heatmap overlaid on a dark map of
                            Nairobi during the evening rush hour.
                            Compared to the morning map, the orange and red
                            hexagons extend further outward from the CBD into
                            surrounding residential neighbourhoods.
                            The overall impression is of broader, more
                            distributed congestion than the morning peak,
                            consistent with commuters dispersing in multiple
                            directions rather than converging on a single
                            centre.
                        </p>
                    </description>
                </image>
            </figure>

            <p>
                There are different ways of observing and summarising the same
                underlying variability in Nairobi journey times.
                Each measurement reveals something the others do not.
                The average gives you a centre; the range tells you how far the
                minimum and maximum are from each other; the heatmap shows you
                variation over time; the spatial map shows you where the
                variation in the city it is concentrated.
                The rest of this chapter introduces the formal measures that
                complement these visual summaries, and considers when to use
                each one.
            </p>

            <insight xml:id="insight-what-you-measure">
                <title>What you measure shapes what you find</title>

                <p>
                    The TomTom figures above include Nairobi's Expressway, which
                    opened in 2022 and cut journey times on routes passing
                    through it from around two hours to roughly 20 minutes.
                    Other sources (including the Express Impress estimate of a
                    30-minute to two-hour range for a specific surface-road
                    journey in Nairobi) are measuring routes that do not use the
                    Expressway at all.
                    The apparent variability in journey times is different
                    depending on which roads you include.
                </p>

                <p>
                    This is a general principle that applies to any dataset: the
                    variation you observe depends partly on what is actually
                    varying, and partly on what you chose to measure and what
                    you left out.
                    Keeping that in mind when comparing summaries from different
                    sources is important in data analysis.
                </p>
            </insight>

            <p>
                <term>Sources:</term> TomTom Traffic Index 2025.
                Nairobi city profile.
                Available at:
                <url href="https://www.tomtom.com/traffic-index/city/nairobi/" visual="tomtom.com/traffic-index/city/nairobi"/>.
                Japan International Cooperation Agency (JICA) (2013).
                <em>The Project on Integrated Urban Development Master Plan 
  for the City of Nairobi in the Republic of Kenya: Household Travel 
  Survey 2013.</em>
                Tokyo: JICA.
                Salon, D.
                and Gulyani, S.
                (2019).
                <q>Commuting in Urban Kenya: Unpacking Travel Demand in Large and 
  Small Kenyan Cities.</q>
                <em>Sustainability</em>, 11(14), 3823.
                <url href="https://doi.org/10.3390/su11143823"/>.
                CC BY 3.0.
            </p>
        </subsection>
    </section>

